# Line endings are part of the product, not an editor preference.
#
# `ProjectTemplate/**` and `Packs/**` are embedded verbatim into GigaClaw.Core.dll and written
# byte-for-byte into every workspace. Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so without
# this file a Windows clone checks those files out with CRLF, the build embeds the CRLF bytes, and
# a workspace initialized on Windows gets different content than the same commit produces anywhere
# else. That is what made CoreInitManifestTests fail on Windows across all 115 of its opaque files
# at once — and, since shipped `.py` content is executed, an OS-dependent payload is a product bug
# rather than a test-fixture nuisance.
#
# Declaring it repo-wide rather than only for the two shipped trees keeps committed baselines,
# golden manifests and fixtures comparable by bytes on every platform, which is what those anchors
# are for. CI asserts it per-platform before it compares any hashes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two formats that genuinely need CRLF: cmd.exe is unreliable with LF-only batch files.
# Neither ships inside a pack.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# text=auto already detects these, but being explicit keeps a heuristic from ever rewriting a
# binary asset.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.db binary

